<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniel Coxe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1640">1640</a>–<a href="/years/1730">1730</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniel Coxe was an English physician to Charles II and Queen Anne and a speculator in colonial land. In 1687 he bought the proprietary rights to West Jersey from the heirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-byllynge">Edward Byllynge</a> and styled himself governor, though he never visited the colony. In 1692 he sold most of his interest to the West Jersey Society, a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> investors.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daniel Coxe was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the oldest of thirteen children of a father also called Daniel Coxe, who came from Stoke Newington and died in 1686. The family traced its line to a Daniel Coxe of Somersetshire in the 13th century who had taken a doctorate of medicine at Salerno University. The younger Daniel was educated at Jesus College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he became a doctor of medicine in 1669. On 12 May 1671 he married Rebecca Coldham, the only surviving child and heiress of John Coldham of Tooting Graveney, an alderman of London.</p><p>He was a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> and a member of the Royal College of Physicians. Samuel Pepys's diary entry of 3 May 1665 describes him poisoning a cat with tobacco oil at Gresham College.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Coxe began buying land in West Jersey in the mid-1680s and served nominally as its governor from 1687 to 1688 and from 1689 to 1692, without ever leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He opened the earliest commercial-scale pottery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. He also bought land in the Mississippi Valley and tried, without success, to settle a colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>.</p><p>In 1698 he acquired Carolana, a grant first made by Charles I to Sir Robert Heath, buying it from Sir James Shaen or his son Arthur; Shaen had obtained the rights from Henry Howard, 6th Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>. The Carolana holding stayed with the Coxe family until 1769, when it was exchanged for land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> valley of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Coxe died in 1730 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>; his portrait is held by the Royal College of Physicians. His son, Colonel Daniel Coxe (1673–1739), lived in the American colonies from 1702 to 1716 and in 1722 published A Description of the English Province of Carolana. In 1731 the son claimed a title superior to that of the West Jersey Society through a deed his father had recorded years earlier, and the courts upheld it. Hundreds of families had to buy their own property again or be evicted; there were lawsuits and riots, and many residents of Hopewell left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, some founding the Jersey Settlement on the Yadkin River in what was then Rowan County.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daniel Coxe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Byllynge's death the physician <b>Daniel Coxe</b> bought the governing rights in 1687 and sold them to the West Jersey Society in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-jersey">West Jersey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1698" aria-label="England on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Daniel Coxe"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 5597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 5597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1698">What was here in 1698? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
